## Decision

This record proposes replacing [0001, subject-only merging](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/).
That accepted record remains unchanged. Match reports by cause, using tenant locale,
booking-form version and report channel. A matching subject is a clue, not a merge rule.
Unknown tenants require investigation rather than an automatic merge.

## What #4471 contains

The table below deduplicates report IDs, selects #4471 and runs the exported cause classifier
against the tenant directory. Counts come from data at render time, not browser-locale tags.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

**Shared parser:** day-first tenants enter day/month dates but the shared parser reads
month/day. This affects app and email reports, booking-v2 forms, and non-form reports from
booking-v3 tenants. For example, R-41203 says “Booked for 2 December. The invite says 12 February.”
Fix the parser's date contract and test ambiguous dates using the tenant's format.

**Booking-v3 hint:** its form displays MM/DD/YYYY for day-first tenants, while the form
interprets input in the tenant's order. This is a separate input-hint defect. Alder Physio,
Copper Studio and Pale Harbour run booking-v3. Fix the hint and input interpretation together;
do not assume a shared-parser fix will repair this route.

Keep these as separate issues. The export's intake tags are guesses based on browser locale,
so their totals do not measure these causes. The source has overlapping export pages; one ID
counts once. Reports are not a count of unique users or independent booking events.

## The queue did not start in July

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The first receipt is in April. The large early-July bar records merges of older reports,
not a July outbreak. Receipt and merge timestamps answer different questions. Weekly buckets
use the calendar date written in each timestamp, without timezone conversion; original
numeric offsets remain in the source export. Booking dates quoted in bodies are separate.

## Exceptions and triage

The desk flags R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344 and R-41620 as one-day-move readings.
The table footnotes them within their tenant-derived group. Their wording does not by itself
prove a third timezone defect. Investigate these reports separately before promising that a
parser fix resolves every symptom; preserve their IDs and original wording.

## How to apply this

Read the tenant's configuration, channel and body before merging. Confirm the same cause
with `sameCause` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`; use the existing dedupe explorer to compare cases.
Keep the parser work in its own ticket and give the hint defect its own ticket. Link both from
the incident, review stale triage guidance, and refresh the live tables as the queue changes.

The old incident's “one issue, one fix” statement is superseded by this analysis.

## Engineering outcome

The course desk update records **#4471 closed 05 oct**. It reports the parser as **fixed**,
with “fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales”. It records the hint as **won't fix** because
the **booking-v3 form retired**. These are different outcomes: closing the parent ticket does
not mean both defects were fixed. The update gives no time or timezone, and no explicit year.

Our [parser ticket](https://github.com/msalewski/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
closes as completed; our [hint ticket](https://github.com/msalewski/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)
closes as not planned. The report counts above remain historical counts from the export,
not reports of an active booking-v3 form. This proposal changes triage policy; it does not
claim that policy 0001 has already been formally replaced.

